17592186072761 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072761 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f9903a21-0217-58f2-8d63-41d105a30c08" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072762 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{4}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072762 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072762 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "223379f8-ee6a-5c47-8bd1-f452e3187e1b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072763 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{2}{3}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072763 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186072763 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "712639d1-5da2-5392-8851-f92f6c470e4e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073495 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073495 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/4f/4f5bfdd38a8c02510e86279ac548a2a6b889f9eb33a8ef8c273fd2e384e54b72.png)\n\nWhat multiplication problem is represented by the model above?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073495 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f26ed088-6f0b-56b8-9027-9b9af4bcf415" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073495 #edb/ref 1074 "We have $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ on the left and $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/b4/b43d5a0e0a44e6d418a901dc9fa806079b1b9425fc111640439e4338f8bf0d84.png)\n\nTherefore, the multiplication problem shown is:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3} \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073498 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14490" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073498 #edb/ref 1071 "What number is missing from the multiplication problem below?\n\n![](images/69/69f859c61c693f7ece01fef8f829fdc772acae77e90be97b7b093fb9849acc95.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073498 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a653f45f-e638-5646-a55c-13b319e511b0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073498 #edb/ref 1074 "We have $\\dfrac{1}{2}$ on the left and $\\dfrac{1}{6}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/dc/dcb89325ddd68043c9edf2f595a7a7d89b3ba078bbb1805af7bea58867f627b6.png)\n\nThe shape on the right-hand side is split into $\\color{blue}3$ equal parts by horizontal lines. So, the shaded part on the left is $\\color{blue}3$ times larger than the shaded part on the right. \n\nTherefore, the multiplication problem shown is:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{2} \\times \\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}{3}} = \\dfrac{1}{6}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{blue}3.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073499 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14491" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073499 #edb/ref 1071 "What picture is missing from the multiplication model below?\n\n![](images/39/39c8668bd4bf5309b708769a99d9d3fa2184f0937411d437de8a1195853e5118.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073499 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9bc02e5b-5028-5aa1-85d8-c1217b425c36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073499 #edb/ref 1074 "In the model, we have $\\dfrac{1}{4}$ on the left.\n\nSince we are multiplying by $\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}5}$, we need to split the whole into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts horizontally.\n\n![](images/65/6584c694fbbc990dfa3b58a19545d3deb9889f162da6e02a9820310a190ae04e.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents $\\dfrac{1}{20}$ of the whole.\n\nThe missing picture is:\n\n![](images/e3/e349233ebb5e3927f0d4bda98cb2470c1f17574a522d2f4b9cbddaf365fe946d.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073500 #edb/ref 1069 "e-14492" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073500 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/82/82439d51448a442dede4b3965ecbbd2f2daa7b2365ea75c80951478fd10af65c.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate $\\dfrac{4}{5} \\times \\dfrac{1}{3}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073500 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4730b433-9614-58ea-a452-e404c237ba3b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073500 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we need to multiply by $\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}3}$, we split the whole into $\\color{blue}3$ equal parts horizontally.\n\n![](images/a9/a95b378064e6ec2ed15637fd05ea49e5f988bfb30d8ae788a7b2086ff920a65f.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents $\\dfrac{4}{15}$ of the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{5} \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{4}{15}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073684 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049804 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073684 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074423 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073684 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074520 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073684 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425828 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073684 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425830 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073684 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying a Multiplication Problem Corresponding to a Model" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073684 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073495 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073684 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "6ffe13c6-a2b6-5d70-8ebe-d152ae4db35f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073685 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074421 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073685 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074422 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073685 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425827 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073685 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073685 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying a Missing Number in a Model" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073685 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073685 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "3f3ddce4-2cf8-5bf6-86d3-8c304ee6c241" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073686 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074394 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073686 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074395 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073686 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425831 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073686 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073686 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying a Model Corresponding to a Multiplication Problem" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073686 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073686 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "0bd1c12d-acb6-5961-a248-47f172fe5bef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073687 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074393 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073687 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186074420 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073687 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425829 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073687 #edb/ref 1039 "Multiplying a Fraction by a Unit Fraction Using a Model" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073687 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186073500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073687 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "16e2265e-5c75-538f-91a1-9120d2f77c9e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073983 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073983 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073983 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073983 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073983 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186074557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073983 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073983 #edb/ref 1045 "Multiplying Fractions by Unit Fractions Using Models" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073983 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186074553 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073983 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073983 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186073983 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "e5d4d32a-c5b1-5bb9-a109-158be8e0c828" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074420 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186072758 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074420 #edb/ref 1069 "q-60224" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074420 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074420 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/7d/7d2474d5fd25cbb23c534e4d8f67726177988a49f9c0e271b91b0ff677dabd41.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate $\\frac{2}{3}\\times \\frac{1}{4}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074420 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d8028372-de90-5318-b125-be12ab64e3cc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074420 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we need to multiply by $\\frac{1}{4}$, we split the whole into $4$ equal parts horizontally.\n\n![](images/41/416c66bc1591d9215bd5e74c975f6e17992ae0d3456262091d48910b9d89abc9.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we obtain that the shaded part represents $\\frac{2}{12}$ of the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\frac{2}{3}\\times \\frac{1}{4}=\\frac{2}{12}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074553 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074554 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074553 #edb/ref 1061 111265 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074553 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1a9dc867-e32f-56af-8da8-b49a7c78e295" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074553 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186075055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074554 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074555 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074554 #edb/ref 1061 111266 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074554 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6a6025d9-8b62-55fc-9986-d44a56cc355f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074554 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073684 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074555 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074556 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074555 #edb/ref 1061 111269 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074555 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "81f70a32-6a53-5fdb-9bfe-968569e95d68" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074555 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073685 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074556 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186074557 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074556 #edb/ref 1061 111272 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074556 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "cbcedc93-87eb-515c-aaed-d451e57961fa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074556 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073686 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074557 #edb/ref 1061 111275 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074557 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "1fd007c8-be54-595a-bb9b-500f99e68515" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186074557 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186073687 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075055 #edb/ref 1064 "We can use fraction models to understand how to multiply fractions.\n\nFor example, let's suppose that we wish to compute the following product of fractions:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3} \\times \\dfrac{1}{2}\n$$\n\nLet's start by representing $\\dfrac23$ using a square fraction model, like the one below. Notice that we have split the fraction *vertically*.\n\n![](images/dc/dc72f1bc6250e2ca39cf424847d3e592858d8cd6765cade9d357a139bbd906b0.png)\n\nSince we need to multiply the above fraction by $\\dfrac{1}{\\color{blue}2}$, we split the whole into $\\color{blue}2$ equal parts *horizontally*. This way, each of the original pieces is split in half. Then, we only keep the shaded pieces in the bottom row.\n\n![](images/41/41d47349763b17ed06777b732997ab4f229088b38252c398854a92a1d62ccb6a.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we see that the shaded part represents $\\dfrac{2}{6}$ of the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3} \\times \\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{2}{6}.\n$$\n\nNotice that we can simplify this answer further by dividing the numerator and denominator by $2.$ However, in this lesson, we'll focus just on multiplication and not worry about simplifying the final answers." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075055 #edb/ref 1065 8959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075055 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7d4c2f3b-ffc4-5837-b2b2-961166892aef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186075055 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425789 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{12}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425789 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425789 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "15321b92-66dd-5be5-aeba-0321bfb68de6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425790 #edb/ref 1080 "\\frac{1}{5}\\times \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{2}{10}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425790 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425790 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "156eeca8-010b-5a5d-8f2e-9ae2f2d453e8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425791 #edb/ref 1080 "4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186425791 #edb/ref 1082 #ed